WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:02.835
-Our next guest is
an Emmy Award-winning actor

00:00:02.835 --> 00:00:04.704
who spent 12 seasons
as one of the stars

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:06.038
of the hit series
"Two and a Half Men."

00:00:06.038 --> 00:00:07.773
His new comedy,
"Extended Family,"

00:00:07.773 --> 00:00:10.076
airs Tuesday nights
at 8:30 here on NBC.

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:11.210
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.278
-Look, I was going
to sleep on this, Julia,

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:14.414
but I'm afraid we have an issue.

00:00:14.414 --> 00:00:15.982
I got pulled over
for speeding tonight.

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:19.685
-So you meant you have an issue.
[ Laughs ]

00:00:19.685 --> 00:00:21.687
Sorry I'm laughing.
It's happening involuntarily.

00:00:21.687 --> 00:00:24.256
How much was the ticket?

00:00:24.256 --> 00:00:25.591
-I got off with a warning.

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:27.093
-Did you cry and tell them
that your mom died?

00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:29.395
That's what I do.

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:31.931
Come on.
Where's the victory smile?

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:33.365
-My victory smile
is in hibernation,

00:00:33.365 --> 00:00:35.902
because we still have an issue,
an issue named Trey.

00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:37.436
-How is Trey the issue?

00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:41.007
-Your fiance got me off.

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:43.476
-Out. Trey got me
out of the ticket.

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:46.179
-Please welcome back to
the show, Jon Cryer, everybody.

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:49.182
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:49.182 --> 00:01:02.862 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:04.196
Welcome back.

00:01:04.196 --> 00:01:06.732
-Thank you, thank you.
Glad to be back.

00:01:06.732 --> 00:01:10.036
-This is a very novel
approach to a sitcom,

00:01:10.036 --> 00:01:12.438
because it's about
a divorced couple

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:16.242
that is still amicable,
and historically in sitcoms,

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:17.777
not only do divorced
couples not get along,

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:19.445
most married couples
don't even like each other.

00:01:19.445 --> 00:01:22.815
-Exactly, exactly. That's what
we thought was novel about it.

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:24.150
But it's fun.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.919
But it's actually
inspired by the true story

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:29.055
that the owner
of the Boston Celtics,

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:33.026
he and his wife share an
apartment with her ex-husband.

00:01:33.026 --> 00:01:34.994
Oh, wow.
-Yeah.

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:36.529
Really thing.
Real thing that happened.

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:38.263
They did it on purpose.

00:01:38.263 --> 00:01:40.099
But it's great, actually,

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:42.935
because they are
very good friends.

00:01:42.935 --> 00:01:45.704
They did that

00:01:45.704 --> 00:01:47.540
so that the kids didn't have
to move back and forth.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:49.275
-Yeah.
-Which I thought was very novel.

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:50.743
Obviously,
it's not for everybody.

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:53.012
Not everybody can do that.
-Uh-huh. [ Laughs ]

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:57.517
But what the show is about is,
is people trying

00:01:57.517 --> 00:02:02.187
to maintain that bit
of friendship that they had

00:02:02.187 --> 00:02:03.722
that brought them together
in the first place.

00:02:03.722 --> 00:02:06.025
-And your own parents,

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:09.561
they got divorced and also
had a very nice relationship.

00:02:09.561 --> 00:02:11.130
-Yes. Thank you for --

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:12.298
Yeah.
-You're very welcome.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:13.833
You know what?

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:15.301
Thank them.

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:18.003
-I will. I will,
now that you mention it.

00:02:18.003 --> 00:02:19.472
No, actually, yeah.

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:22.274
They were divorced when I was
four, and my dad remarried.

00:02:22.274 --> 00:02:25.545
My mom and my stepmother
are great friends and hang out.

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:28.548
My half-brothers and sisters
lived in in my mom's house

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:29.615
when they moved to New York.

00:02:29.615 --> 00:02:32.485
I mean,
it's crazy hippie [bleep].

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:33.953
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:37.022
Sorry.

00:02:37.022 --> 00:02:38.824
But it can work.
It can be great.

00:02:38.824 --> 00:02:41.561
And that's
the show we wanted to make.

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:43.029
-You mentioned
the hippie part of it.

00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:44.230
Both your parents
were stage actors.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:45.398
-Yes.

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:48.834
-And so was that
always the plan for you?

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:50.904
-Yeah, it was always
kind of in the background,

00:02:50.904 --> 00:02:52.438
because I loved being backstage.

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:54.774
Backstage is just this crazy,
you know -- if you ever get

00:02:54.774 --> 00:03:00.046
a chance to do theater,
do it, because --

00:03:00.046 --> 00:03:01.447
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:02.882
Well, no, because
you get backstage,

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:04.817
and it's this wonderful people
and these big personalities

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:06.986
and they're ridiculous,
and I just love that.

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:11.223
But I also sort of wanted
to be an astronaut.

00:03:11.223 --> 00:03:13.926
But it was really interesting,

00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:15.694
because after
"Two and a Half Men" ended,

00:03:15.694 --> 00:03:20.700
I got this request to host an
episode of a documentary series

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.203
where I would investigate
the Russian space program.

00:03:24.203 --> 00:03:25.938
And I said, "Oh, well,
that sounds fun.

00:03:25.938 --> 00:03:28.741
Will you fly me to Moscow

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:29.975
and let me train
to be an astronaut?"

00:03:29.975 --> 00:03:32.344
And they said, "Sure."
-Wow.

00:03:32.344 --> 00:03:37.150
-So I flew to Moscow
and trained to be an astronaut.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.651
For how long?
-Just for a couple of weeks.

00:03:39.651 --> 00:03:41.154
-Okay. That's enough.

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:43.322
-That's really all you need.

00:03:43.322 --> 00:03:46.392
But what I learned was,

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:48.294
I would not be
a very good astronaut.

00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:49.362
-Oh, interesting.

00:03:49.362 --> 00:03:50.897
-Yes, because they don't --

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:53.465
What they don't tell you is,
you know, you see the movies,

00:03:53.465 --> 00:03:54.667
and it's just brave people

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:56.435
doing brave things,
and they look all cool,

00:03:56.435 --> 00:03:58.738
and everything seems
super competent.

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:02.742
But once you start realizing

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:05.444
that there's certain things

00:04:05.444 --> 00:04:08.714
that you have to have a level of
comfort with that you might not.

00:04:08.714 --> 00:04:10.349
-Okay. What sort of things
are we talking about?

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:12.518
-Like in the Russian spacecraft

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:14.687
that they send you
to the ISS in,

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:16.088
and they still do this.

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:19.058
They still use the same old ones
that they used back in the '60s.

00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:20.793
-Wow.
-Swear to God, they still do.

00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:22.662
And they're about this big.

00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:25.364
And they jam
three people into that,

00:04:25.364 --> 00:04:28.101
and nobody gets a window
or anything.

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:32.271
One guy or gal
gets a thing called a visor,

00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:35.107
where they, it's a thing
that they look through

00:04:35.107 --> 00:04:38.878
to make sure that the spacecraft
is on the right attitude.

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:40.446
But what they don't tell you

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:43.216
is the visor goes up
between your legs,

00:04:43.216 --> 00:04:46.219
and you are jammed like this
wearing a space suit

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:51.323
with two other people right next
to you in something this big,

00:04:51.323 --> 00:04:53.226
and then they put you
on top of a rocket

00:04:53.226 --> 00:04:55.595
and shoot you out
of the Earth's orbit.

00:04:55.595 --> 00:04:57.763
-God.

00:04:57.763 --> 00:05:00.266
-The movies
don't have it like that.

00:05:00.266 --> 00:05:02.969
-Yeah.
-"Star Trek" is not like that.

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:04.537
Um, so...

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:07.506
so I realized pretty quickly.

00:05:07.506 --> 00:05:09.175
And I did the centrifuge.

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:11.344
I did all that, where
it pushes back your face

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:14.379
and all that stuff,
and I felt so badass.

00:05:14.379 --> 00:05:16.516
I also did a thing --

00:05:16.516 --> 00:05:19.285
They have, their spacesuits,

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:21.987
you know, when you go
outside of the spacecraft,

00:05:21.987 --> 00:05:24.090
theirs are different
than the Americans.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.659
They have a door on the back
of their spacesuits

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:30.630
that is -- it's hinged,
and it's like,

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:33.198
you just step in through,
the backpack opens,

00:05:33.198 --> 00:05:35.334
and there's the spacesuit,
and you put your legs through,

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:39.605
and then they just go slam,
and slam you inside this thing.

00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:41.740
And another thing
they don't tell you,

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:42.875
you know, when you're wearing
a spacesuit in the movies,

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:44.109
it looks all cool.

00:05:44.109 --> 00:05:45.645
You look like
the Stay Puft Marshmallow Man.

00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:47.980
-Yeah, of course.
-How how hard could that be?

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.016
What they don't tell you is,
it's a pressure suit,

00:05:50.016 --> 00:05:52.685
so they have to
pump it full of air.

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:55.588
And so you're inflated,
basically.

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:59.625
You're like those guys
that wave at the car wash.

00:05:59.625 --> 00:06:01.026
-Yeah. The car wash blow up guy.

00:06:01.026 --> 00:06:03.362
-Except yes, you get less
freedom of movement, though.

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:04.430
So you're just ksshhh!

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:08.000
And then just
doing anything at all

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.702
is this incredible effort.

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:11.903
And I was like, you know what?

00:06:11.903 --> 00:06:13.673
Being an astronaut
requires effort.

00:06:13.673 --> 00:06:15.374
No, thank you.
-Yeah.

00:06:15.374 --> 00:06:18.077
It feels like if I watch
this documentary,

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:20.846
the takeaway would be,
being an astronaut sucks.

00:06:20.846 --> 00:06:21.980
That seems to be --
-Yes.

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:23.249
That's the title of it,
actually.

00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:25.050
That's what we ended up with.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:26.819
But it was interesting to,
something that I had

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:29.155
so many dreams
about as a kid, to go,

00:06:29.155 --> 00:06:31.924
"Oh, you know what? No.
No, thanks."

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:35.361
-Well, another dream
for a lot of kids, maybe,

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:37.363
in "Supergirl,"
you got to be Lex Luthor.

00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:38.563
-Yes.
-He's a badass.

00:06:38.563 --> 00:06:39.898
-He's a total badass.

00:06:39.898 --> 00:06:41.200
-So in the same way
that you were saying

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.770
astronauts are badass,
and then you actually do it,

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:45.838
and you find out
it's not badass at all,

00:06:45.838 --> 00:06:49.242
did you have the same moment
when you got to play Lex Luthor?

00:06:49.242 --> 00:06:50.543
-Um...

00:06:50.543 --> 00:06:53.179
[ Laughter ]

00:06:53.179 --> 00:06:55.313
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:55.313 --> 00:06:58.016
Thank you. Thank you.

00:06:58.016 --> 00:07:00.452
I'm all about being scary,
I think.

00:07:00.452 --> 00:07:04.289
-That's real scary.
That's the scariest Lex Luthor.

00:07:04.289 --> 00:07:07.426
"Heyyy, Supergirl."

00:07:07.426 --> 00:07:08.627
[ Both laugh ]

00:07:08.627 --> 00:07:12.231
-That wasn't exactly --
-"Heyyy, Supergirl!"

00:07:12.231 --> 00:07:15.601
-That wasn't my reading,
just so you know.

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:19.705
But no, actually,
we were about to do a stunt,

00:07:19.705 --> 00:07:21.974
which is very manly and badass.

00:07:21.974 --> 00:07:24.543
I was hanging from
a helicopter in green screen,

00:07:24.543 --> 00:07:27.112
so you're about a foot
and a half off the ground.

00:07:27.112 --> 00:07:31.417
But it looked great
in the end, Seth.

00:07:31.417 --> 00:07:33.919
-You were in "Superman 4"?
-Yes.

00:07:33.919 --> 00:07:36.155
-Lenny was your character.

00:07:36.155 --> 00:07:37.590
-I was Lenny Luthor.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.658
I played my own nephew.

00:07:39.658 --> 00:07:41.060
It's like inception.

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:44.729
-So you must have been
a very young actor at the time.

00:07:44.729 --> 00:07:46.499
-Yeah, yeah. I was 21.

00:07:46.499 --> 00:07:48.935
-Okay,
so you're in "Superman 4."

00:07:48.935 --> 00:07:52.304
And did you learn
anything as a young actor

00:07:52.304 --> 00:07:54.039
doing a giant superhero movie?

00:07:54.039 --> 00:07:58.144
-Oh, well, there's a lot of
flying in "Superman 4."

00:07:58.144 --> 00:07:59.412
-One of the main parts.
-It's a Superman movie.

00:07:59.412 --> 00:08:01.813
Exactly.
And I was so excited about that.

00:08:01.813 --> 00:08:03.849
You see the movies, you know,
like, in the first movie,

00:08:03.849 --> 00:08:05.451
Superman, they --

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:07.320
it was beautiful flying,
it was balletic.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.054
-Yeah.
-I get there on the first day,

00:08:09.054 --> 00:08:10.590
and the stunt coordinator
is the guy

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:13.526
who's a legend
in the area of flying stuff.

00:08:13.526 --> 00:08:15.928
He's amazing, a British guy.

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:19.131
And he says, "Okay, one thing
you have to be aware of --"

00:08:19.131 --> 00:08:21.133
I'm not doing a good
British accent at all.

00:08:21.133 --> 00:08:25.504
"Is that when they
put the harness on you" --

00:08:25.504 --> 00:08:27.473
this is 1987, I think --

00:08:27.473 --> 00:08:30.409
"When they put
the harness on you,

00:08:30.409 --> 00:08:32.445
they have to cinch it
incredibly tight,

00:08:32.445 --> 00:08:37.682
so it will catch any hair
that you have on there."

00:08:37.682 --> 00:08:39.084
So he said, "You may want

00:08:39.084 --> 00:08:40.585
to take care of any hair
that you have,

00:08:40.585 --> 00:08:44.090
either on the back
or on the front, either way,

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:46.225
so that it doesn't catch
in the harness.

00:08:46.225 --> 00:08:51.230
And I thought, oh, okay.
So I got rid of everything.

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:53.999
Nair came into play.

00:08:53.999 --> 00:08:59.604
Nair is a very specific '80s
reference for you guys.

00:08:59.604 --> 00:09:04.309
And did the thing,
and smooth as glass, I did.

00:09:04.309 --> 00:09:05.677
I was very excited.

00:09:05.677 --> 00:09:10.349
I mean, both the shot
and my --

00:09:10.349 --> 00:09:13.385
-Regions.
-Regions.

00:09:13.385 --> 00:09:17.656
But what I did not realize
was that the painful stuff is,

00:09:17.656 --> 00:09:20.159
you know, is delayed,
because what I learned is,

00:09:20.159 --> 00:09:23.395
when your butt hair grows back,
it hurts.

00:09:23.395 --> 00:09:24.530
-Oh!
-Yes.

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:25.731
This is just --
I don't know if any--

00:09:25.731 --> 00:09:29.201
I hope America does not
have to experience this,

00:09:29.201 --> 00:09:31.904
but when your your
butt hair grows back,

00:09:31.904 --> 00:09:34.707
it is no fun for anyone.

00:09:34.707 --> 00:09:38.977
And actually, Melissa Benoist,
who was Supergirl,

00:09:38.977 --> 00:09:40.779
the first day of work, I said,

00:09:40.779 --> 00:09:43.816
"Hey, I know we're going to be
doing some flying stuff.

00:09:43.816 --> 00:09:46.652
Do we still have to do that?"

00:09:46.652 --> 00:09:49.155
And she was like, "No!"

00:09:49.155 --> 00:09:52.658
This guy was clearly
pulling a prank, apparently.

00:09:52.658 --> 00:09:56.928
[ Laughter ]

00:09:56.928 --> 00:09:58.964
-Pretty good prank.
-It's a pretty good prank.

00:09:58.964 --> 00:10:01.300
Pretty good.
-Pretty good prank.

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:02.634
-Yep. He got me good.
-There you go.

00:10:02.634 --> 00:10:04.503
Well, thank you
for being with us and sharing.

00:10:04.503 --> 00:10:06.438
I mean, I think
if there was a PSA,

00:10:06.438 --> 00:10:08.874
it's like, don't be an astronaut
and never shave your butt hair.

00:10:08.874 --> 00:10:10.209
-Yes.

00:10:10.209 --> 00:10:12.344
-Thank you. Thank you so much.
-Thank you.

00:10:12.344 --> 00:10:13.512
-Jon Cryer, everybody.

00:10:13.512 --> 00:10:16.215
[ Applause ]

00:10:16.215 --> 00:10:17.917
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

